# Files this repo deliberately does NOT carry from its template.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. Every repo in this tree merges from a `stem` remote. A file
# that a descendant has deleted is not a settled decision as far as git is
# concerned: the next time the template TOUCHES that file, the cascade stops
# with a modify/delete conflict, and whoever is running it has to work out from
# scratch whether the deletion was deliberate. That has already happened
# repeatedly for `sending-indicator.e2e.ts` in bleeps and mandalas, and the only
# record of the intent was a commit message from months earlier.
#
# Listing the path here does not change what git does - the conflict still
# happens - but it makes the answer a lookup instead of an excavation, and
# `scripts/apply-omissions.sh` turns the resolution into one command.
# `web/test/offshoot-omissions.test.ts` fails if any listed path comes back, so
# a cascade that silently reintroduces one cannot be committed quietly.
#
# FORMAT. One path per line, relative to the repo root. `#` starts a comment.
# Give every entry a reason: the next person's question is never "which file",
# it is "why", and an unexplained list is how a deletion outlives the reason for
# it and becomes cargo cult.
#
# THIS LINE USED TO SAY "this template omits nothing - it is where the files come
# from", AND IN THIS REPO IT WAS FALSE TWICE OVER: this is a GAME, not a template,
# and the four entries directly below it are things it omits. It arrived by
# inheritance from template-commit-reveal, where it was also false, and sat here
# above the list contradicting it - which is the defect
# `an-inherited-paragraph-cannot-say-this-repo` names, in the file whose whole job
# is to be read during a merge. Upstream has its own corrected version now,
# naming ITS stem; this is ours, naming ours.
#
# Files here come from `template-commit-reveal`, and what this repo omits is
# THE TEMPLATE'S GAME: its e2e suites, its fixture, and every unit test it writes
# for `$lib/placement`. Each is listed below with what replaced it.
#
# UPSTREAM'S OWN TWO ENTRIES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. It lists
# `web/src/routes/demo` and `web/src/routes/offline-demo`, which are jolly-roger's
# app demo - and our stem does not have them either, so they can never arrive from
# it. An entry for a path the stem does not carry is an assertion that passes
# forever while meaning nothing, which is the state this list must not drift into.
# Take an entry from upstream only when the path exists in OUR stem.

web/e2e/tests/game.e2e.ts
# The PARENT TEMPLATE'S GAME, not this one. It plans a placement on a CELL,
# bonds a stake on that cell, and asserts the cell's `totalStake` moved. This
# game has no cells and no per-cell stake: avatars occupy positions, a plan is a
# sequence of enter/move/exit, and THE BOND IS THE AVATAR - one held in the
# contract's custody.
#
# Its second test asserted what a missed reveal COST. Nothing here charges for
# one and nothing is going to: `MissedRevealState` is Unknown | Clear | Blocked
# | Acknowledging | Failed, and credits are not a stake - they are abstracted
# GAS, the signer's balance denominated in actions, so there is no cost to
# report the way the template forfeits a bonded reserve. A missed reveal here
# blocks play until it is acknowledged, and that is the whole of it.
#
# REPLACED BY web/e2e/tests/board.e2e.ts, which plays the same three beats
# (plan, commit, reveal) against this game: an avatar enters the world, the
# commitment hides where until the reveal, and only the reveal puts it on the
# board. Written on its own path rather than over this one, so the template can
# go on changing its own suite without ever conflicting with ours.

